What is OpenTelemetry (OTel) and why is everyone talking about it?


OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework under the CNCF that’s quickly becoming the industry standard for collecting and exporting telemetry data — metrics, logs, and traces — from applications and systems.
Instead of juggling multiple vendor-specific agents and risking vendor lock-in, OTel provides a single, vendor-neutral way to instrument applications.


 Why OpenTelemetry matters:

𝐔𝐧𝐢𝐟𝐢𝐞𝐝 𝐎𝐛𝐬𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲 → Collects metrics (latency, CPU, throughput), traces (end-to-end request tracking), and logs (event details) in one framework.

𝐅𝐥𝐞𝐱𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐈𝐧𝐬𝐭𝐫𝐮𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 → Use language SDKs (Java, Python, Go, Node.js, etc.) or auto-instrumentation without touching your code.

𝐁𝐚𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐧𝐝-𝐀𝐠𝐧𝐨𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐜 → Export telemetry to Prometheus, Grafana, Jaeger, Zipkin, Datadog, Splunk, New Relic, and more.

𝐒𝐭𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐚𝐫𝐝𝐢𝐳𝐞𝐝 𝐀𝐏𝐈𝐬 → Built on OpenTracing + OpenCensus, ensuring consistency across tools and languages.

𝐂𝐥𝐨𝐮𝐝-𝐍𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐯𝐞 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐝𝐲 → Designed for Kubernetes and microservices where observability is non-negotiable.


